.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-COUNT-OBJECTS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-count-objects \- count a bucket's objects, optionally under a prefix
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm count-objects
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIPREFIX\fR]
[\fB\-\-include\-directories\fR|\fB\-d\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Counts the objects in a bucket. The figure is exact at the moment of
asking, and can be narrowed to a key prefix.
.SH WHICH OF THE TWO COUNTS TO ASK FOR
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-object-count (1)
answers the same question differently, and the difference is worth
knowing before choosing:
.TP
.B count-objects
Runs a query. Exact, can be narrowed to a prefix, and costs what
counting a bucket's objects costs \- which on a bucket of a million is
not nothing.
.TP
.B get-object-count
Answers the bucket's stored
.I objects
figure, a running total kept as objects come and go. One document read
whatever the bucket holds, always the whole bucket, and only as current
as the last time the monitoring module recomputed it \- every
.I euclid.modules.emo.bucket\-count\-period
seconds.
.PP
Ask this one when the answer has to be right, or has to be about part of
a bucket. Ask that one when it has to be cheap, or is being polled.
.SH DIRECTORIES
A directory in a bucket is a zero-byte object whose key ends in a slash,
which is what lets an empty one exist at all. It is not counted by
default \- that matches what a listing shows and what the bucket's own
.I objects
figure counts, since a directory is not something a client stored.
.B \-\-include\-directories
counts them, which is what to use when comparing against a listing taken
with directories included.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME\fR
Bucket name. A full ERN also works, and is what reaches another
namespace. Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix " " \fIPREFIX\fR
Only count objects whose key starts with this. A prefix is matched
literally and is not a glob; it names a "directory" only by the
convention that keys contain slashes. Defaults to empty, which counts
the whole bucket.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-include\-directories
Count the markers that stand for directories too.
.PP
The bucket may be given by
.B name
instead of by ERN, in which case it is resolved in your session's account
and namespace; see
.B NAMES AND ERNS
in
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A summary
.RI ( "{ern, prefix, includeDirectories, count}" )
is printed as JSON. The prefix is echoed back because a bare number does
not say what it counted.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, no bucket
of that name exists, or the caller was not granted that bucket. A bucket
that is not there is a failure rather than a count of zero \- the two
would otherwise be indistinguishable, and a typo would read as an empty
bucket.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Everything in the bucket
euclid-cli esm count-objects --bucket dropbox

# Just one supplier's inbox
euclid-cli esm count-objects --bucket dropbox --prefix jvo/incoming/

# What a listing with directories included would show
euclid-cli esm count-objects --bucket dropbox --include-directories

# Did the purge finish?
euclid-cli esm count-objects --bucket dropbox --prefix old/
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-object-count (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-objects (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-bucket-size (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-purge-bucket (1)
